<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borsippa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Birs Nimrud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borsippa was an ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, about 20 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It was the cult centre of the god Nabu, whose temple, Ezida, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>. The ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> at the site, Birs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, was once thought to be the Tower of Babel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> settled around the city in the 1st millennium BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Borsippa is an archaeological site in Babylon Governorate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, built on both sides of a lake on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, southwest of Babylon and about 15 kilometres from the ancient site of Dilbat. Its ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> is among the most clearly identifiable in the region. Later Arabic tradition identified it with the Tower of Babel, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> called it the Tower of Borsippa, or tongue tower, in a stele found on the site in the 19th century. Modern scholarship concludes that its Babylonian builders raised it as a religious edifice in honour of the local god Nabu, called the son of Babylon's Marduk, as befitted Babylon's lesser sister city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The tutelary god of Borsippa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period was Tutu, whom the prologue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> names as the god of the city. A later inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> dedicates the Ezida temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, showing Tutu being absorbed by him, and Ezida later became the home of Nabu. Ibbi-Sin's governor Puzur-Tutu is known from the closing years of Ur III. Year names of the Babylonian rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apil-sin">Apil-Sin</a> record that one entered Borsippa and the other built its city wall. The Kassite ruler Marduk-apla-iddina I rebuilt Ezida, and Marduk-shapik-zeri restored it.</p><p>Borsippa depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and was never the seat of a regional power. From the ninth century BCE it lay on the borderland south of which were the tribal houses of Chaldea. It had a large scribal class in the first millennium BCE. The temple of Nabu was destroyed in 484 BCE during the suppression of a revolt against the Achaemenid emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>. Nebuchadnezzar II's inscription tells how he restored the temple of Nabu with bricks of noble lapis lazuli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Henry Creswicke Rawlinson directed work at Borsippa in 1854, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> excavated Ezida for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> from 1879 to 1881. In the 1890s looters took about 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, mostly from Ezida. An Austrian team from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> dug for sixteen seasons between 1980 and 2003 and found that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> had a core of unbaked bricks 60 by 60 metres, with a baked-brick mantle taking it to 78 by 78 metres, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>'s tower encased a smaller tower of the second millennium BCE. Rising in seven terraces, it reached 70 metres, and even in ruin it stands 52 metres above the plain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borsippa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a second and possibly a third stele were found with it, and over 50 manuscripts of the laws are known from Susa, Babylon, Nineveh, Assur, <b>Borsippa</b>, Nippur, Sippar, Ur, Larsa and other sites; together they fill in most of the stele's text, including much of the erased section.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry Rawlinson announced the find to the Royal Asiatic Society on 17 November 1879 and wrongly named <b>Borsippa</b> as its source.</blockquote><a href="/work/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid king Antiochus I restored the temple of the god Nabu at <b>Borsippa</b> and commemorated the work in Akkadian cuneiform, in the manner of the Babylonian kings before him.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Bit-Dakkuri, 'House of Dakkuru', was one of the leading Chaldean tribes of Babylonia, settled along the Euphrates between Babylon and <b>Borsippa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-dakkuri">Bit-Dakkuri</a></li></ul></section>
